---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { kpOrBeyond, locations, model, regionOf } from '../lib/site';

const sorted = [...locations].sort((a, b) => Math.abs(b.mlat) - Math.abs(a.mlat));
const steps = model.kpBoundary.map((edge, kp) => ({ kp, edge }));
const bom = [
  { kp: 3, limit: 'Southern Tasmania' },
  { kp: 4, limit: 'All of Tasmania' },
  { kp: 5, limit: 'Coastline of Victoria' },
  { kp: 6, limit: 'Southwest coast of Western Australia' },
  { kp: 7, limit: 'Southern New South Wales' },
  { kp: 8, limit: 'Southern Queensland' },
  { kp: 9, limit: 'The tropics' },
];
---

<Base
  title="What Kp Do You Need to See the Aurora in Australia and NZ?"
  description={`The Kp index needed to see the aurora australis from ${locations.length} places, from Kp 4 in southern Tasmania and Southland to Kp 9 in Sydney, with the working shown.`}
  path="/kp-needed/"
>
  <h1>What Kp do you need to see the aurora?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    It depends on how far you are from the magnetic pole. Southern Tasmania and the far south of New Zealand need about
    Kp 4. Melbourne needs about Kp 7. Sydney needs Kp 9.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Place</th>
          <th scope="col">Magnetic latitude</th>
          <th scope="col">Faint, on camera</th>
          <th scope="col">Visible</th>
          <th scope="col">Overhead</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {sorted.map((l) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              <a href={`/forecast/${l.slug}/`}>{l.name}</a>
              <span class="sub">{l.area === regionOf(l).name ? l.area : `${l.area}, ${l.country === 'NZ' ? 'New Zealand' : 'Australia'}`}</span>
            </th>
            <td>{Math.abs(l.mlat).toFixed(1)}°S</td>
            <td>{kpOrBeyond(l.kpFaint)}</td>
            <td>{kpOrBeyond(l.kpVisible)}</td>
            <td>{kpOrBeyond(l.kpOverhead)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Sorted from closest to the aurora to furthest. Magnetic latitude is AACGM-v2 for {model.epoch}. "Beyond Kp 9" means
    the rule of thumb below never brings the aurora within range, however strong the storm.
  </p>

  <h2>What the three columns mean</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Faint, on camera.</strong> The aurora's edge is within about 1000 km. NOAA says a bright aurora can be seen
        from that far when conditions are right. In practice this is a glow low on the horizon that a long exposure
        catches and the eye usually misses.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Visible.</strong> The edge is within {model.easyMarginDeg} degrees of magnetic latitude, the range in which
        NOAA says the aurora is easily seen. This is the level the go or no-go on this site treats as enough.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Overhead.</strong> The edge has reached the place itself. The aurora can fill much of the sky.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>The working</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      NOAA describes the aurora as a ring around the magnetic pole whose outer edge sits near 66 degrees of magnetic
      latitude when Kp is 0 and moves about 2 degrees towards the equator with each step, reaching 48 degrees at Kp 9.
      The step-by-step values used here are:
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Kp</th>{steps.map((s) => <th scope="col">{s.kp}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Edge of the aurora</th>{steps.map((s) => <td>{s.edge}°</td>)}</tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Take a place's magnetic latitude, add {model.easyMarginDeg} degrees, and find the first Kp whose edge is at or inside
      that. Hobart is at 53.4°S. Add 5 and you get 58.4. The edge is at 58.3 for Kp 4, so Hobart needs about Kp 4.
      The camera column adds {model.farMarginDeg} degrees instead, which is 1000 km expressed as latitude.
    </p>
    <p>
      NOAA reports Kp in thirds: 3.67 is "4 minus" and 4.33 is "4 plus". This site rounds to the nearest whole number,
      the same way NOAA counts 4.67 as Kp 5 for its G1 storm level.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A second opinion from the Bureau of Meteorology</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The Bureau's Space Weather Services publishes its own table of the northern limit of aurora visibility for each
      Kp. It is the outer limit in good conditions, so it lines up with the "faint, on camera" column here, not the
      "visible" one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Kp</th><th scope="col">Bureau's northern limit of visibility</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {bom.map((row) => (
          <tr><th scope="row">{row.kp}</th><td>{row.limit}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      From Tasmania to southern New South Wales the two agree to within one Kp step. For Queensland the Bureau's table
      is more generous than the rule used here, which keeps Brisbane out of range. Source:
      <a href="https://www.sws.bom.gov.au/Aurora/1/1">Bureau of Meteorology, Space Weather Services</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>How often does each level happen?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      NOAA's storm scale gives a feel for it over an 11-year solar cycle: G1 conditions (Kp 5) on about 900 days, G3
      (Kp 7) on about 130 days and G5 (Kp 9) on about 4 days. They are not spread evenly: storms rise and fall with the
      Sun's cycle, and both NOAA and the Bureau of Meteorology point to the weeks around the March and September
      equinoxes as the likeliest time of year.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kp is also a three-hour average for the whole planet. A short, sharp burst can light the sky while Kp is still
      catching up, and a high Kp in daylight is no use to anyone. That is why the pages for each place look at the
      dark hours only and show the live readings alongside. See <a href="/reading-the-numbers/">reading the numbers</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
